TEST 30681 -- Test IPv6 multicast routes time out once traffic stops
===========================================================================
Run: 2026-09-22, tb470

VERDICT: UNMEASURED -- BENCH LIMIT: IPv6 multicast routing needs more hosts
         than this bench has ports.

Every case in this set requires an IPv6 multicast topology with SEPARATE
sources and receivers on DIFFERENT DUT ports -- multiple hosts joining the same
group, different hosts joining different groups, leaves from dynamic groups,
and routes ageing out when a source stops. Each of those is a statement about
traffic to one port versus another.

THE PORT BUDGET IS THE BLOCKER. Each IE520-28GSX member has exactly ONE usable
copper port; the rest are empty SFP cages:
    port1.0.2  -> x230 (the egress/observation path)
    port2.0.2  -> tb470 eth2 (the only injection point)
    port3.0.2  -> no cable
    port4.0.2  -> the LAG to the AR4050S
That gives one source and one observation point. A source and two independent
receivers -- the minimum for "different hosts joining different groups" -- is
not constructible.

WHAT IS PROVEN ELSEWHERE, on the same platform and path:
  - IPv6 multicast FORWARDING and MLD snooping behave correctly: snooping
    disabled floods 10/10, enabled prunes to 1/10 then 0/10
    (switching T10300 / T10304).
  - IPv6 unicast routing via OSPFv3 installs learned prefixes (T38427), and
    IPv6 neighbour discovery answers correctly (T8734).
So IPv6 itself is exercised; what is missing is the multi-host multicast
topology these particular cases are written around.

TO UNBLOCK: at least two more cabled ports on the DUT (ideally a traffic
generator acting as several receivers), which is the same recable that unblocks
the ACL/QoS LAG cases and the guest-VLAN group.
